Using `localStorage.getItem('authToken')` in this example encourages storing long‑lived authentication tokens in `localStorage`, which is readable by any JavaScript running in the page. If an attacker ever achieves XSS in this app (via any future bug or third‑party script), they can exfiltrate the bearer token from `localStorage` and perform authenticated actions or account takeover. In the docs, prefer patterns that keep tokens out of web‑accessible storage (for example, HttpOnly cookies or a short‑lived in‑memory token managed by a dedicated auth layer) rather than recommending `localStorage` for secrets.
```suggestion
// Implement this in your auth layer. Avoid storing tokens in localStorage/sessionStorage;
// prefer HttpOnly cookies or short-lived in-memory tokens.
declare function getAuthToken(): string | undefined;

const dataSource = createObjectStackAdapter({
  baseUrl: 'https://api.yourcompany.com',
  token: getAuthToken()
```

This production configuration example again reads the bearer token from `localStorage`, implying that authentication secrets are persisted there. Because `localStorage` is fully accessible to JavaScript, any future XSS vulnerability in the app (including from third‑party scripts) would allow an attacker to steal this token and impersonate users, even across sessions. Update the guidance to use an auth mechanism that keeps tokens out of JavaScript‑accessible storage (for example, secure HttpOnly cookies or a dedicated auth client that stores tokens only in memory) so new integrations do not adopt this unsafe pattern.
```suggestion
import { authClient } from './authClient';

const dataSource = createObjectStackAdapter({
  baseUrl: process.env.VITE_API_URL || 'https://api.production.com',
  // Auth token is obtained from an in-memory auth client; do not persist it in localStorage/sessionStorage.
  token: authClient.getAccessToken(),
```
